Cosmo
=====

Configurable Opinionated System for Modular Options (Cosmo) is an attempt at
proving that it is possible to bridge NixOS's declarative systems with the
end-user interactivity of other Linux distros and OSes.
Cosmo is a work-in-progress proof of concept.

Original Ideas and Views
========================

To read about what inspired this (and what this project is), read REPORT.

Features Status
===============

This project is meant to be a proof-of-concept or minimal viable product (MVP).
There are multiple aspects that should be implemented in order to prove it
works from end to end in a complete environment.

Necessary for PoC
-----------------

This proof of concept is a work-in-progress and will keep that status for as
long as the following features have not been implemented.

COSMO is conceptually divided into two layers: the engine (this repository) and
the distro/UX (the future orchestrator and GUI).

This repository implements the Engine MVP. Its scope is strictly limited to 
the translation layer between standard JSON data and NixOS module evaluation.

Engine MVP (scope of this repo)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

* (DONE) Data bridge: A Nix flake architecture that cleanly ingests a static
  JSON file to conditionally build a complete NixOS system.
* (DONE) Schema extraction: A pure-Nix engine that safely evaluates the module
  tree and exports a strict JSON Schema Draft 7 without infinite recursion.
* (DONE) Locality of behavior: Module design where NixOS implementation and
  JSON schema definition live in the exact same file.
* (DONE) Overriding: Built-in support for standard Nix files (overrides.nix) to
  cleanly bypass the JSON bridge for power users.
* (TODO) Schema translation keys: Separating the schema strings (titles, 
  descriptions) from the evaluation tree to support i18n localization.

Distro vision (outside the scope of this repo)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The following features are required for the complete COSMO system, but will
be implemented in separate client/orchestrator repositories:

* (TODO) Frontend client: A stateless GUI application that dynamically renders
  its interface entirely from the extracted JSON schema, and writes user
  selections back to cosmo.json. Basically a settings app.
* (TODO) VCS integration: A daemon or script that automatically stages and
  commits the JSON configuration to a local Git repository before each system
  rebuild, ensuring rollback capability.
* (TODO) The "magic file" architecture: A packaging mechanism that bundles the
  declarative git repository (cosmo.json, flake.lock) alongside an imperative
  snapshot of the user's state (~/.config, dconf dumps) into a single, portable
  .cosmo archive.
* (TODO) State migration hooks: A system to safely migrate or wipe imperative
  user configuration files when the underlying declarative software is changed
  (e.g., swapping PulseAudio for Pipewire).
  This is not Home Manager. It would remove old configuration information that
  is not relevant on newer versions. This feature is the closest we are to
  falling into the AST trap like many similar projects before us.

Necessary for Implementation
----------------------------

The following features are optional for a proof of concept but would be
necessary for a fully-fledged implementation.

* A complete formatter and linter set using treefmt and git hooks
* A CI, on the simpler side of what CI/CD can offer. We are not evaluating
  100k+ packages like in nixpkgs, so a complete system like OfBorg would be
  overkill, but something like GitHub Actions checking the flake would come a
  long way.
* End-to-end testing, the Nix way with 'checks'. This is basically necessary.
* Generous binary caching. One could imagine caching most of the default
  settings, as users would not usually do crazy edits like the Nix community
  does, except power users, which are already used to building on their PCs.
* Bulletproof file structure, to avoid having to move files later
* First-class rules and contribution guidelines (like NixOS's RFCs)
* First-class commits and PRs conventions that are understandable by machines
  and scripts (like NixOS's RFCs).
* First-class documentation. I'm not talking about setting up an auto Nix docs
  website and calling it a day, I'm talking real documentation understandable
  by humans.
* A website with features exposed for end users to refer to.
* Translations support for schema. Ideally separating the schema from the
  translation keys and serving them separately

